Z||000020||Formula88||11-07-2001||04:21 PM||||Racing for titles is <B>always</B> a bad idea, IMO.  What if your car stalls?  Ignition module goes south?  What if he's got a ringer he's not telling you about, like a hugh NOS bottle that may well blow his engine, but still win the race?  What if he runs you off the road?  Rules don't mean anything when you're doing something illegal in the first place.  How about you do it at a local drag strip's grudge night?  That way you have proof of who wins.  Of course, if you get over-anxious and red-light, there goes your Fiero.<P>Go ahead and race, but if you win, you know darn well you won't get the car.  He's too much talk to keep his word.  If he wins, you either go back on your word and keep your car, or you keep your word and loose your car.  There's really not much to win here, except bragging rights.  Race for $100, or $1000, or whatever both parties are willing to front.  Key word being "front."  If they ain't got it at race time, you don't have a chance of winning it regardless of who's faster.<P>If you race for titles, both people should bring their title with them and have a 3rd party hold them.  You're talking a lot of money when you are betting your car.  Most people will do anyting to keep from having to pay.<P>And if you think it's a sure thing, you are ripe to be played for a sucker.  I am positive you will win, IF it's a stock RAV4 and you have no problems.  If not, well, how much is your Fiero worth to you?||||reg||4
